# Dockerfile.dockerignore — build context filter for demo/6_adk_telegram_bot.
#
# Docker picks this up automatically because it sits next to the Dockerfile and
# is named <Dockerfile>.dockerignore (BuildKit 1.6+, stable since Docker 25;
# verified here on Docker 29.4.0 / buildx 0.33.0). It does NOT replace a
# root-level .dockerignore — building with an explicit -f is what activates it.
#
# The context is the repository root, which holds .git, scratch checkouts under
# tmp/, and apps/.env with real provider and bot credentials. None of it is
# reachable from the packages this binary imports.
#
# One rule about the syntax cost real debugging time, so it is written down here:
#
#   A negated DIRECTORY pattern re-admits everything under it. A leading `**`
#   matches at every depth, and a parent's own entry governs its children, so
#   `!demo/` does not admit one folder — it admits every demo in the tree.
#   Name the exact directory instead, as below.
#
# Verified: the resulting context is go.mod, go.sum, the three internal packages
# and demo/6_adk_telegram_bot — and apps/.env is not among them.

# Exclude everything, then re-admit only what the build needs. An allowlist is
# the safe direction: a file added later is out by default, rather than silently
# shipped into a layer.
**
!go.mod
!go.sum

# The three internal packages the bot imports. A prefix entry re-admits its
# subtree, which is what is wanted here.
!internal/adkenv/
!internal/labrun/
!internal/modelcfg/

!demo/6_adk_telegram_bot/

# Defence in depth for the one file that must never reach a layer, a build cache
# or a registry. apps/.env is already git-ignored and already excluded by the
# rules above; this line means a future `!apps/` cannot quietly undo that.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Not compiled, and not needed to build the binary, so the context stays the
# source that actually ships.
**/*_test.go
**/README.md
